},{camera:`player`},{fade:`in`}],outcome:[{flag:oM(`woodcutting`)}]}},{after:`act1-spring-fair`,event:{id:`act1-hazel-animals`,kind:`main`,story:hz,title:`Hazel's Hens`,requires:[{done:`act1-spring-fair`},{daysSince:{event:`act1-spring-fair`,min:1}}],trigger:{talk:`hazel`},lead:`Hazel promised to set you up with animals for the Fair. Find her at Penrose Ranch.`,summary:`Hazel taught you to keep animals and gave you hay to start. She sells hens and cows; they arrive the next morning.`,hints:[{from:`edda`,text:`Hazel has a crate of pullets out with your name chalked on it. Go and see her before she gives them to someone with more sense.`},{from:`kit`,text:`Hazel says you're getting chickens! Can I name one? I want to call it Captain.`}],cutscene:[{say:`hazel`,text:`[happy] Farmer! Good, you came. Right. Animals. Lesson one, and don't roll your eyes.`},{say:`hazel`,text:`Hens live in a coop, and Odette's old one in your pasture is still sound. Cows need a barn: Clara Corran can put one up for you.`},{say:`hazel`,text:`Fill their feeder with hay every evening, or they go to bed hungry and sulk.`},{say:`hazel`,text:`Talk to them every day. Brush the cows, milk them once they trust you. Happy animals give better eggs and milk, whatever anyone says.`},{say:`hazel`,text:`[laugh] Here, some hay to start you off. When you want hens or cows, come and order them here. They come over the next morning.`},{do:{give:`hay`,qty:10}},{do:{friendship:`hazel`,points:30}},{say:`hazel`,text:`And I want one of your hens at the Fair. Prize Hen, Thistledown. Has a ring to it.`}],outcome:[{flag:oM(`livestock`)}]}},{after:`act1-hazel-animals`,event:{id:`act1-noticeboard`,kind:`main`,story:hz,title:`The Noticeboard`,requires:[{done:`act1-spring-fair`},{daysSince:{event:`act1-spring-fair`,min:3}}],trigger:{talk:`bram`},lead:`Bram has been asking after you in Willowmere.`,summary:`Bram put the noticeboard on the square back to work. Pay for a project there and Clara builds it over a few nights.`,hints:[{from:`clara`,text:`Bram's been measuring the old noticeboard on the square all morning. Either he's mending it or he's planning something. Hard to tell with him.`}],cutscene:[{say:`bram`,text:`Ah, there you are. I'll be honest, I didn't think Hazel's Fair would get past talk. Now Nell is baking and Odo is mending the racecourse rails.`},{say:`bram`,text:`[sad] It set me thinking. The waterwheel at Brightwater has not turned in years. The lighthouse lamp is dark. We stopped mending things, somewhere along the way.`},{say:`bram`,text:`So I have put the noticeboard on the square back to work. Village projects: what needs doing, and what it costs.`},{say:`bram`,text:`Clara Corran will build anything on it, if someone brings the gold and the materials. Lumber, mostly. You have an axe, I believe.`},{say:`bram`,text:`[happy] Start with the waterwheel, if you ask me. Ivy Brightwater has wanted power in her workshop since she could walk.`},{do:{friendship:`bram`,points:30}}],outcome:[{flag:oM(`projects`)}]}},{after:`act2-tell-lia`,event:{id:`act2-quarry-vote`,kind:`main`,story:`Act 2: Tidecall`,title:`The Vote`,requires:[{done:`act2-tell-lia`},{daysSince:{event:`act2-tell-lia`,min:2}}],trigger:{talk:`bram`},lead:`The village wants to talk about opening the old quarry up at Hollowdeep. Bram has the last word on it.`,summary:`The village voted, and Bram took the boards off the old quarry. Odette's pickaxe waits in the crate by the mouth.`,hints:[{from:`odo`,text:`Forty years that quarry has been boarded up. Good copper down there. Somebody ought to ask Bram why it stays shut.`},{from:`maren`,text:`If the quarry ever opens, I can put proper heads on your tools. Copper, iron. I'm sick of mending hinges.`}],cutscene:[{say:`bram`,text:`[sad] You've heard, then. Odo has been going door to door about the old quarry up the mountain.`},{say:`bram`,text:`I nailed those boards up myself, after the collapse at Hollowdeep. I told everyone it was for safety. It was. Mostly.`},{say:`narrator`,text:`That evening half the village crowds into the Town Hall. Odo speaks for the quarry and Maren for her forge. When the hands go up, Bram counts them twice.`},{say:`bram`,text:`[sad] ...The quarry opens. I have had the boards taken down. Be careful down there.`},{say:`bram`,text:`Odette's old pickaxe is in the crate by the mouth. One rock on each level hides the way down, and Maren will put better heads on your tools if you bring her ore.`}],outcome:[{flag:oM(`mining`)}]}},{after:`act2-quarry-vote`,event:{id:`act2-hazel-mare`,kind:`side`,story:`Hazel's Mare`,title:`Marigold`,requires:[{done:`act2-quarry-vote`},{daysSince:{event:`act2-quarry-vote`,min:3}},{done:`act1-hazel-animals`},{hearts:{villager:`hazel`,min:5}},{built:`farm-barn`}],trigger:{talk:`hazel`},lead:`Hazel has something at Penrose Ranch she wants to show you, and she is being cagey about it.`,summary:`Hazel gave you Marigold, her dad's old mare. She lives by your barn: press Use beside her to ride, and Use again to get down.`,hints:[{from:`edda`,text:`[sad] Hazel's been out in the paddock with the old mare every evening this week. She's deciding something. Go and let her decide it at you.`},{from:`kit`,text:`Hazel's horse is bored! She told me. She needs someone with a really long road. You've got loads of roads.`}],cutscene:[{say:`hazel`,text:`[shy] Farmer. Got a minute? Come round the back. No, don't look at me like that, it's nothing bad.`},{say:`narrator`,text:`Behind the ranch house a tall chestnut mare lifts her head from the hay rack and blows at Hazel, as if they have been arguing.`},{say:`hazel`,text:`This is Marigold. She was Dad's. When he and Mum took the ferry he couldn't take her, so she stayed. Like me.`},{say:`hazel`,text:`[sad] She's fourteen and fit as a fiddle, and she's bored out of her skull. I've got cows to milk. I can't ride her the way she wants.`},{say:`hazel`,text:`You've got a barn now, and half the island to cover. Bram says you're up at Hollowdeep and back before the bakery shuts.`},{choice:[{label:`Are you sure? She was your dad's.`,then:[{say:`hazel`,text:`[happy] That's exactly why. She should be going somewhere, not standing here waiting for a ferry that isn't coming.`},{do:{friendship:`hazel`,points:60}}]},{label:`I'll take good care of her.`,then:[{say:`hazel`,text:`[laugh] You will, or you'll answer to me. And to her. She bites people she doesn't like. Not you, though. She likes you. I checked.`},{do:{friendship:`hazel`,points:40}}]}]},{say:`hazel`,text:`I'll walk her over to your barn tonight. Hold the reins, don't kick, and she'll take you anywhere a road goes. Hold Run and she'll really go.`},{say:`narrator`,text:`Marigold waits by your barn. Press Use beside her to climb on, and Use again to get down. Leave her anywhere and she finds her own way home.`}],outcome:[{flag:oM(`riding`)}]}}],_z=`Act 1: Roots of the Veil`,vz=`Act 2: Tidecall`,yz=[-14.39,36.1],bz={from:[-7.2,35.4],look:[-13.4,33.6],height:2.3,lookHeight:.8},xz=[{id:`act1-whole-blanket`,kind:`main`,story:_z,title:`Blue, Red and White`,requires:[{done:`act1-edda-blanket`},{daysSince:{event:`act1-edda-blanket`,min:1}}],trigger:{talk:`edda`},lead:`Edda wants to show you the whole blanket. Visit her at Penrose Ranch.`,summary:`Edda's blanket: spirals in bluebell blue, roots in poppy red, the wing in moon daisy white. She gave you seeds.`,hints:[{from:`hazel`,text:`Gran's been airing out that old blanket all morning. She'll be wanting you. Brace yourself, there's a story with every stitch.`}],cutscene:[{say:`edda`,text:`[happy] There you are. I dug it out of the chest last night. Help me spread it out, my arms aren't what they were.`},{say:`narrator`,text:`Edda unrolls a long blanket, soft with age. Around its border runs a ring of seven shapes: the same ones carved on the stones behind your farm.`},{say:`edda`,text:`Spirals I always stitch in blue. My mother dyed that thread with bluebells. Roots go in red, poppy red. And the wing in white, for her bird.`},{say:`edda`,text:`Seven shapes in a ring, seven stones in a ring. And seven beds in front of them, I'd wager. Flowers in the right colours, in the right beds.`},{say:`player`,text:`Which bed comes first?`},{say:`edda`,text:`[sad] That I never knew. The pattern goes round and round, it has no beginning. If anyone worked that out, it was your grandmother.`},{say:`edda`,text:`[happy] Here. Bluebells, poppies and moon daisies, saved from my own garden. Fen sells more if they don't take.`},{do:{give:`bluebell-seeds`,qty:4}},{do:{give:`poppy-seeds`,qty:4}},{do:{give:`moon-daisy-seeds`,qty:2}},{do:{friendship:`edda`,points:30}}],outcome:[{flag:`knows:blanket`}]},{id:`act1-ring-riddle`,kind:`main`,story:_z,title:`Where Her Bird Rests`,requires:{done:`act1-whole-blanket`},when:{near:`stone-ring`},trigger:`auto`,lead:`Take Edda's colours back to the stone ring behind the farm.`,summary:`Another torn page from Odette's journal: "Begin where her bird rests, and walk on with the sun."`,cutscene:[{actor:`heron`,at:yz,height:.45,face:`player`},{camera:bz},{say:`narrator`,text:`The heron was waiting on the broken stone at the back of the ring. As you came near, it bent its long neck and tugged something out of the briars at its feet.`},{say:`narrator`,text:`A torn page, weathered but still readable, in Odette's hand.`},{page:`Seven beds for seven stones,
her colours, one by one.
Begin where her bird rests,
and walk on with the sun.`},{say:`player`,text:`With the sun. East, then south, then west...`},{say:`narrator`,text:`In front of each stone, under the briars, someone had once turned the soil in a ring. Seven beds, just as Edda said.`},{camera:`player`}],outcome:[{flag:`knows:ring-order`}]},{id:`act1-root-shrine-wakes`,kind:`main`,story:_z,title:`The Root Shrine Wakes`,requires:{done:`act1-ring-riddle`},when:[{flag:`ring:complete`},{near:`stone-ring`},{hour:[19,4]}],trigger:`auto`,lead:`Plant the Flower Ring in Edda's colours, beginning where the heron rests and walking with the sun. The stones may answer after dark.`,summary:`The Root Shrine woke at nightfall, and somewhere a woman laughed. A new page: "Tides hold what the land forgets."`,hints:[{from:`edda`,text:`Spirals blue, roots red, the wing white. If a flower wilts, the ring didn't want it there. Roll up your sleeves and try again.`,when:{not:{flag:`ring:complete`}}},{from:`hazel`,text:`Gran says if you plant the old stones wrong, the flowers just keel over. Fen's got more seeds, I checked.`,when:{not:{flag:`ring:complete`}}},{from:`wren`,text:`Walking with the sun? In the old texts that means sunwise: east, then south, then west. The way a clock's hands go, on a map with north at the top.`,when:{not:{flag:`ring:complete`}}},{from:{spot:`stone-ring`},text:`Seven bare beds before seven stones, each stone carved with a spiral, a root or a wing. The heron watches from the broken stone at the back.`,when:{not:{flag:`ring:complete`}}},{from:{spot:`stone-ring`},text:`All seven beds are in bloom. The stones are quiet, but the air feels like it is holding its breath. Odette always said the island was most awake at night.`,when:{flag:`ring:complete`}},{from:`kit`,text:`Was that your farm with the lights last night? Little green ones, round the old stones. Or I dreamed it.`,when:{flag:`ring:complete`}}],cutscene:[{actor:`heron`,at:yz,height:.45,face:[-13,33]},{camera:bz},{say:`narrator`,text:`As the last light left the sky, the flowers in the beds began to glow, faintly, like embers in a grate.`},{fx:`root-shrine-wake`},{fx:`heron-glow`},{say:`narrator`,text:`One by one the glyphs woke, spiral, root and wing, until the whole ring shone. The heron spread its wings and glowed with them.`},{say:`narrator`,text:`And for a moment, from nowhere and everywhere, you heard a woman laughing. Warm, and a little tired, as if at a very old joke.`},{say:`player`,text:`[surprised] ...Grandma?`},{walk:`heron`,to:[-30,50],speed:6,height:12,wait:!1},{wait:1.2},{say:`narrator`,text:`When the glow settled, a torn journal page lay on the centre stone.`},{page:`Tides hold what the land forgets.`},{fade:`out`},{exit:`heron`},{camera:`player`},{title:`The Root Shrine`,subtitle:`The mist has pulled back from the road to Gullwing Cove`},{fade:`in`}],outcome:[{flag:`shrine:root`},{unlock:`coast`},{act:`act2`}]},{id:`act2-lia-lights`,kind:`main`,story:vz,title:`Lights Under the Water`,requires:[{act:`act2`},{daysSince:{event:`act1-root-shrine-wakes`,min:1}}],trigger:{talk:`lia`},lead:`The torn page spoke of tides. Lia Tamsin knows the sea better than anyone: find her by the pier at Gullwing Cove.`,summary:`Lia told you about the four Tidestones on the point: the sea must run through every pool, then out by the old channel.`,hints:[{from:`nell`,text:`[sad] My Lia's been staring out at the bay again. Something's on her mind. Talk to her, would you? She won't talk to me.`},{from:`bram`,text:`Tides? Ask Lia Tamsin. That girl reads the sea the way Wren reads books.`},{from:{spot:`bay-shore`},text:`Far out in the bay, the tops of a ring of standing stones break the water. The tide never seems to drop far enough to reach them.`}],cutscene:[{say:`lia`,text:`[surprised] Oh. Hi. Sorry, I was miles away. Do you ever look at the bay at night?`},{say:`lia`,text:`Out past the pier there's a ring of old stones under the water. Some nights there are lights down there. Blue ones. Moving.`},{say:`player`,text:`Odette's journal says tides hold what the land forgets.`},{say:`lia`,text:`[surprised] ...Gran Tamsin used to say the old keepers could turn the tide off those stones. With the tidestones: the four in the pools out on the point.`},{say:`lia`,text:`The sea has to pass through every pool before it'll let go of the shrine, she said, and then run out the old channel into the bay. And the stones are chained together under the rock, so turning one drags another round.`},{say:`lia`,text:`[shy] I always thought it was a story for kids. But you've got that look. The same one Odette used to get.`},{choice:[{label:`Come out to the point with me.`,flag:`lia-asked-along`,then:[{say:`lia`,text:`[laugh] Not a chance, it's slippery as anything out there. But come and tell me what you find.`}]},{label:`Why haven't you gone to look?`,flag:`lia-asked-why`,then:[{say:`lia`,text:`[sad] Because if it's real, I'd have to stay. And I've been half packed for a year.`}]}]},{say:`lia`,text:`[happy] Oh, and if you're going to be poking round the water, you should know how to fish. Odette kept her rod on the rack by your farmhouse door. I used to borrow it.`},{say:`lia`,text:`Hold it, face the water and cast. When something bites, reel it in, gently. The strand by your farm bites, and so does Maple Pond.`},{do:{friendship:`lia`,points:40}}],outcome:[{flag:oM(`fishing`)}]},{id:`act2-tide-turns`,kind:`main`,story:vz,title:`The Tidestones`,requires:{done:`act2-lia-lights`},when:[{flag:`tide:turned`},{area:`coast`}],trigger:`auto`,lead:`Turn the Tidestones in the pools on Gullwing Point until the sea runs through all four pools and out the old channel to the bay.`,summary:`You turned the Tidestones. The current runs through every pool, and a drowned causeway leads out to the shrine in the bay.`,hints:[{from:`lia`,text:`Watch the water when you turn a stone, it shows you where it's going. And if two stones move at once, that's the chains.`},{from:`wren`,text:`A tidestone? If the carving is an arrow, I would guess it tells the water which way to go. The best old magic is usually simple.`},{from:`kit`,text:`The point? My map has a channel cut in the rock there, running west into the bay. Straight as a ruler. Someone dug it on purpose.`},{from:{spot:`tide-pools`},text:`Four squat stones sit in the tide pools, each carved with a wave pointing one way. The sea rushes into the pool nearest the open water.`}],cutscene:[{camera:{from:[36,74],look:[46,88],height:4,lookHeight:0}},{fx:`tide-turn`},{say:`narrator`,text:`The last stone ground into place. The current ran from pool to pool, bright as glass, and poured out along the old channel into the bay.`},{camera:{from:[-50,2],look:[-58,50],height:3,lookHeight:.4}},{say:`narrator`,text:`Far out in the bay, the water over the drowned stones began to swirl and draw back. Under the surface, a line of flat stepping stones ran from the beach out to the shrine.`},{say:`narrator`,text:`Not clear yet: the daytime tide still covers them. But at night, when the tide runs lowest, the way should open.`},{camera:`player`}],outcome:[{flag:`tide:path`}]},{id:`act2-tide-memory`,kind:`main`,story:vz,title:`Hold On`,requires:{done:`act2-tide-turns`},when:[{near:`tide-shrine`},{hour:[20,6]}],trigger:`auto`,lead:`On the night tide, after 8 pm, the causeway rises. Walk out to the Tide Shrine in the bay.`,summary:`At the Tide Shrine you saw a memory of Odette on the night she vanished: "I'm coming, Mirael. Hold on." She walked into the mist on purpose.`,hints:[{from:`lia`,text:`[surprised] The stepping stones! I saw them from the pier last night, under the moon. After dark the water goes right off them.`},{from:{spot:`bay-shore`},text:`Under the water, a line of stepping stones runs out to the shrine. The tide is too high to cross by day.`,when:{hour:[6,20]}}],cutscene:[{camera:{from:[-61.4,56.6],look:[-55.8,58.4],height:1.6,lookHeight:1.2}},{say:`narrator`,text:`Inside the ring the air was still and cold, and smelled of the deep sea. At your throat, the Veilstone began to glow.`},{fx:`odette-memory`},{say:`narrator`,text:`Mist gathered beside the centre stone and took a shape: a woman in a long coat, a lantern held out before her. Odette, as she was a year ago.`},{say:`narrator`,text:`"I'm coming, Mirael," she said, to someone you couldn't see. "Hold on. Just a little longer."`},{say:`player`,text:`[surprised] Grandma! Wait!`},{say:`narrator`,text:`She turned, as if she had heard something very far away, and smiled. Then she walked into the mist, and the mist folded shut behind her.`},{fx:`odette-memory-end`},{say:`narrator`,text:`The stones around you flickered with blue light, the lights Lia had seen from the shore. The Tide Shrine was awake.`},{fade:`out`},{camera:`player`},{title:`The Tide Shrine`,subtitle:`Odette walked into the Veil on purpose`},{fade:`in`}],outcome:[{flag:`shrine:tide`}]},{id:`act2-tell-lia`,kind:`main`,story:vz,title:`Half Packed`,requires:{done:`act2-tide-memory`},trigger:{talk:`lia`},lead:`Tell Lia what you saw at the Tide Shrine.`,summary:`You told Lia about Odette's memory. She has decided to stay, at least until the Lantern Festival.`,cutscene:[{say:`lia`,text:`[surprised] You went out there. At night. You actually went out there! What did you see?`},{say:`player`,text:`My grandmother. A memory of her, on the night she disappeared. She went into the mist on purpose, Lia. She was going to help someone.`},{say:`lia`,text:`[sad] ...On the night of the Lantern Festival. We floated lanterns from this beach, and she just walked out of the light.`},{say:`lia`,text:`There hasn't been a festival since. Nobody had the heart.`},{say:`lia`,text:`[happy] Maybe it's time there was. I'll unpack. Just a bit. At least until the lanterns are lit.`},{do:{friendship:`lia`,points:60}}],outcome:[{flag:`lia-staying`}]}],Sz=`Prologue: The Letter`,Cz=`Act 1: Roots of the Veil`,wz=[{id:`prologue-arrival`,kind:`main`,story:Sz,title:`Thistledown`,when:{near:`farmyard`},trigger:`auto`,lead:`Make your way to your grandmother's farm.`,summary:`Bram Hollis met you at Thistledown and gave you Odette's keys and her torn journal.`,cutscene:[{fade:`out`},{title:`Prologue`,subtitle:`The Letter`},{say:`narrator`,text:`The fishing boat had been lost in fog for an hour when the captain cut the engine. "No further," he said. "There's nothing out there."`},{say:`narrator`,text:`Then the shell pendant at your throat began to glow: your grandmother's Veilstone. The mist drew back like a curtain, and there was the island.`},{do:{flag:`key:veilstone`}},{actor:`bram`,at:[-1.8,-16],face:[1,2]},{actor:`heron`,at:[-3.4,12.6],height:6.3,face:[1,2]},{camera:{from:[-2.5,2],look:[-1,-8],height:2.4,lookHeight:1.2}},{fade:`in`},{walk:`bram`,to:[.2,-.9],speed:1.3},{face:`bram`,to:`player`},{camera:{from:[-3.8,.6],look:[.6,.4],height:1.9,lookHeight:1.4}},{say:`bram`,text:`[happy] There you are! Odette's grandson, unless I miss my guess. You have her eyes. Bram Hollis, elder of Willowmere. Welcome to Thistledown.`},{say:`bram`,text:`[sad] She went missing a year ago, on the night of the Lantern Festival. We searched for weeks. The letter you carry, she left with me, to send if she didn't come back.`},{say:`bram`,text:`These are hers. The house keys, and her journal. Half the pages are torn out, I am afraid. She was very private about it, near the end.`},{do:{flag:`key:farm-keys`}},{do:{flag:`key:journal`}},{choice:[{label:`I'll look after the farm.`,flag:`arrival-promise`,then:[{say:`bram`,text:`[happy] That's all she would have wanted. The field has gone wild, but the soil is good.`}]},{label:`What happened to her?`,flag:`arrival-asked`,then:[{say:`bram`,text:`[sad] Nobody knows. She walked out toward the old stones with a lantern that night, and in the morning there was only mist.`},{say:`bram`,text:`Clear the field. Plant something. The island likes to be tended; she always said so.`}]}]},{say:`bram`,text:`The village is up the road to the north. Come and meet everyone when you're settled. We don't see many new faces.`},{walk:`bram`,to:[-1.8,-16],speed:1.3,wait:!1},{camera:{from:[3,4.5],look:[-3.4,12.6],height:2.2,lookHeight:5.6}},{say:`narrator`,text:`On the farmhouse roof, a white heron watched you. When you looked back at it, it didn't fly away.`},{fade:`out`},{exit:`bram`},{exit:`heron`},{camera:`player`},{fade:`in`}],outcome:[{meet:`bram`}]},{id:`prologue-mist`,kind:`main`,story:Sz,title:`The Fading`,requires:{done:`prologue-arrival`},when:[{day:{min:3}},{area:`island`},{hour:[19,4]}],trigger:`auto`,lead:`Settle in at the farm. The island has a way of showing itself after dark.`,summary:`On your third night, the far side of the island flickered and vanished into the mist.`,hints:[{from:`kit`,text:`I map the mist walls. They move at night. If you watch from your farm after dark, you'll see. Not tonight, probably. Give it a few days.`},{from:`bram`,text:`[sad] Mind the mist walls after dark. They are restless, some nights.`}],cutscene:[{camera:{from:[4,6],look:[55,-35],height:6,lookHeight:6}},{wait:.6},{say:`narrator`,text:`Something made you look up. Across the island, the far hills shimmered like heat over a road.`},{fx:`mist-flicker`},{say:`narrator`,text:`Then they were gone. Not hidden by cloud: gone, folded into a wall of white mist. A moment later they flickered back, fainter than before.`},{say:`player`,text:`[surprised] ...That's not weather.`},{camera:`player`}]},{id:`prologue-journal`,kind:`main`,story:Sz,title:`The First Page`,requires:{done:`prologue-mist`},when:[{daysSince:{event:`prologue-mist`,min:1}},{area:`island`}],trigger:`auto`,lead:`Get some sleep. Things may make more sense in the morning.`,summary:`A loose page in Odette's journal: "The roots remember. Start where the oldest tree drinks."`,cutscene:[{say:`narrator`,text:`You couldn't stop thinking about the mist. Over breakfast you opened Odette's journal again, and a page you hadn't seen before slid out.`},{page:`The roots remember.
Start where the oldest tree drinks.`},{say:`player`,text:`The oldest tree on the island. Where would that even be?`}]},{id:`prologue-root-shrine`,kind:`main`,story:Sz,title:`The Root Shrine`,requires:{done:`prologue-journal`},when:{near:`stone-ring`},trigger:`auto`,lead:`Find "where the oldest tree drinks", as the journal says.`,summary:`The heron led you to the ring of stones behind the farm: the ruined Root Shrine.`,hints:[{from:`edda`,text:`The oldest tree? There's a twisted thing behind Odette's farm, older than the village. It leans over the old stones like it's drinking from them. She used to sit there for hours.`},{from:`pip`,text:`[surprised] The white bird keeps flying over your farm, out the back, past the house. Toward the creepy stones. I'm not scared of them. I just don't go there.`},{from:`wren`,text:`Odette asked me once what the glyphs on old standing stones might mean. I never did find out which stones she meant.`},{from:{spot:`old-maple`},text:`A grand red maple, its roots drinking from the pond. Lovely, but it can't be much older than the farmhouse. Somewhere there must be an older tree.`}],cutscene:[{actor:`heron`,at:[-2,44],height:8,face:[-14.4,36.1]},{camera:{from:[-8.6,28.2],look:[-12.6,33.6],height:2.6,lookHeight:1.4}},{walk:`heron`,to:[-14.4,36.1],speed:5,height:.45},{face:`heron`,to:`player`},{say:`narrator`,text:`The heron glided down out of the trees and landed on one of the standing stones, as if it had been waiting for you.`},{say:`narrator`,text:`Seven stones in a ring, half lost in briars. Above them leans a twisted tree, roots spilling into the cracked slab at the centre.`},{fx:`heron-glow`},{say:`narrator`,text:`Under the moss, the stones are carved: spirals, roots, a woman with a heron's wings. For a heartbeat the carvings glow, faint as a breath, and the heron glows with them.`},{say:`player`,text:`[surprised] Grandma... what were you doing out here?`},{walk:`heron`,to:[-30,50],speed:6,height:12,wait:!1},{wait:1.2},{fade:`out`},{exit:`heron`},{camera:`player`},{title:`The Root Shrine`,subtitle:`The mist over Bramble Meadows has thinned`},{fade:`in`}],outcome:[{flag:`found:root-shrine`},{unlock:`meadows`},{act:`act1`}]},{id:`act1-spring-fair`,kind:`main`,story:Cz,title:`No Point`,requires:[{act:`act1`},{daysSince:{event:`prologue-root-shrine`,min:1}}],trigger:{talk:`hazel`},lead:`Hazel Penrose has been asking after you. Find her in Willowmere.`,summary:`Hazel wants to bring back the Spring Fair. You said you would help.`,hints:[{from:`bram`,text:`Hazel Penrose was asking after you. Something about the Spring Fair. I told her not to get her hopes up. She took it about as well as you would expect.`},{from:`edda`,text:`My granddaughter has a bee in her cap again. The Fair, the Fair. Go and let her talk at you, it'll save my ears.`},{from:{spot:`village-well`},text:`Someone has chalked SPRING FAIR?? on the rim of the well, in big hopeful letters. Underneath, in another hand: "no point."`}],cutscene:[{say:`hazel`,text:`[happy] There you are! You're the one who's been poking round the old stones, right? Gran says the heron goes with you everywhere.`},{say:`hazel`,text:`[angry] Listen. Fifteen years ago this village had a Spring Fair. Races, prize hens, Nell's pies. Now everyone just says there's no point.`},{say:`hazel`,text:`I want it back. This spring. But I can't do it on my own, and nobody here will lift a finger.`},{choice:[{label:`Count me in.`,flag:`fair-helper`,then:[{say:`hazel`,text:`[laugh] Ha! I knew it. I knew you had some Penrose in you somewhere.`}]},{label:`Why does it matter so much?`,flag:`fair-asked`,then:[{say:`hazel`,text:`[sad] Because if we stop having anything to look forward to, the mist may as well take the lot of us.`},{say:`hazel`,text:`[happy] So. Are you in?`},{say:`player`,text:`I'm in.`}]}]},{say:`hazel`,text:`[happy] Then first things first: you need animals. Come by the ranch once the meadow road's clear and I'll set you up.`},{do:{friendship:`hazel`,points:50}}],outcome:[{flag:`fair-helper`}]},{id:`act1-edda-blanket`,kind:`main`,story:Cz,title:`Edda's Blanket`,requires:{done:`act1-spring-fair`},when:{hearts:{villager:`edda`,min:1}},trigger:{talk:`edda`},lead:`Edda Penrose knows the old patterns, but she doesn't talk about them with strangers. Get to know her (1 heart).`,summary:`Edda recognised the carvings at the Root Shrine: the same pattern she has woven into her blankets all her life.`,hints:[{from:`hazel`,text:`Gran won't talk shrine stuff with strangers. Sit with her, chat every day, bring her something nice. She'll come round. She always does, eventually.`},{from:`wren`,text:`Have you seen Edda's shawl? Those shapes are very like the glyphs in the archive. She says she just likes them.`}],cutscene:[{say:`edda`,text:`[surprised] Hm? Speak up. Ah, it's you. Odette's boy. Sit, sit.`},{say:`player`,text:`The stones behind the farm are carved. Spirals and roots, and a woman with wings.`},{say:`edda`,text:`[surprised] ...Seven stones? Spiral, root, root, spiral, wing?`},{say:`edda`,text:`My mother taught me that pattern, and her mother taught her. I've woven it into every blanket I ever made. I thought it was just a pretty shape.`},{say:`edda`,text:`[sad] Odette used to ask me to sing the old songs while I wove. I thought she was humouring an old woman. Maybe she wasn't.`},{say:`edda`,text:`[happy] Come back tomorrow, once I've dug the whole blanket out of the chest. The beds round those stones were never meant to be empty.`},{do:{flag:`knows:flower-ring`}}]}],Tz={id:`clara`,hearts:[{title:`Measure Twice`,summary:`Clara taught you her father's rule: measure twice, cut once. She does everything the slow, right way.`,cutscene:[{say:`clara`,text:`Hold the end of this tape, would you? Thank you. Again. And once more.`},{say:`player`,text:`That's the third time you've measured it.`},{say:`clara`,text:`[happy] Measure twice, cut once. My father's rule. I measure three times, because I'm worse than he was.`},{choice:[{label:`Can you show me how?`,then:[{say:`narrator`,text:`She showed you how to mark a line with the pencil from behind her ear, square to the edge, so thin you could barely see it.`},{say:`clara`,text:`[happy] Good. Very good. Most people rush that part.`},{do:{friendship:`clara`,points:40}}]},{label:`Seems slow.`,then:[{say:`clara`,text:`It is slow. A boat built fast sinks fast. I'd rather be slow and dry.`}]}]},{say:`clara`,text:`[shy] Thank you for the hand. People here are polite to me. Not many hold the other end of the tape.`}]},{title:`Old Maps`,summary:`Clara showed you her collection of old island maps. The mist walls are drawn differently on every one.`,cutscene:[{say:`clara`,text:`[happy] Come in, come in. Mind the shavings. I want to show you something.`},{say:`narrator`,text:`She unrolled map after map across her workbench, holding the corners down with chisels.`},{say:`clara`,text:`Every map of the island from the last hundred years. Look at the coast. Look at the mist. It's never in the same place twice.`},{say:`clara`,text:`And here. Thistledown. Your farm is on every single one. Even the oldest.`},{choice:[{label:`Mark my farm on your newest one.`,then:[{say:`narrator`,text:`You drew a tiny farmhouse in pencil. Clara added a tiny heron on the roof, very carefully.`},{say:`clara`,text:`[laugh] There. Now it's accurate.`},{do:{friendship:`clara`,points:50}}]},{label:`Why collect them?`,then:[{say:`clara`,text:`[sad] Because things change, and I like to know what was there before. People forget. Paper doesn't.`}]}]}]},{title:`The Corran Girl`,summary:`Someone called Clara "the Corran girl" in the street. She told you she didn't really come back for the timber.`,hints:[{from:`nell`,text:`[sad] Somebody said something unkind to Clara at the inn last night. She left her cider. She never leaves her cider.`}],cutscene:[{say:`clara`,text:`[sad] Oh. It's you. Sorry. I'm fine.`},{say:`clara`,text:`Someone at the inn called me 'the Corran girl' again. I'm thirty-one. I have a name.`},{say:`clara`,text:`[sad] I tell everyone I came back for the cheap timber. That isn't true. I came back because of my father. Because of what people say he did.`},{choice:[{label:`You're Clara. Not 'the Corran girl'.`,then:[{say:`clara`,text:`[shy] ...Clara. Yes. Thank you. That helps more than it should.`},{do:{friendship:`clara`,points:60}}]},{label:`What do people say he did?`,then:[{say:`clara`,text:`[sad] That he cut corners in the mine, and ran. I don't believe it. I can't prove it either. Not yet.`},{do:{friendship:`clara`,points:30}}]}]},{say:`clara`,text:`[happy] I'm glad it was you who found me sulking. You don't make a fuss. I like that.`}]},{title:`Morning Cider`,summary:`Clara shared apple cider with you on the boatshed steps at sunrise, her favourite hour, and said the island feels like home again.`,lead:`Clara loves mornings. Find her early, before 10 (8 hearts).`,when:{hour:[6,10]},cutscene:[{say:`clara`,text:`[happy] You're up early. Good. Sit. I saved you a cup.`},{say:`narrator`,text:`Warm apple cider, the sun just over the water, and sawdust drifting gold in the light from the workshop door.`},{say:`clara`,text:`This is my favourite hour. Before anyone needs anything. Before the saw starts.`},{say:`clara`,text:`[shy] When I came back, the island felt like somewhere I was visiting. Lately it feels like home. I think that's you.`},{choice:[{label:`I feel the same.`,then:[{say:`clara`,text:`[shy] ...Good. I measured that three times before I said it, you know.`},{do:{friendship:`clara`,points:50}}]},{label:`Hold her hand.`,then:[{say:`clara`,text:`[surprised] Oh. ...Oh. Your hands are cold. Keep them there anyway.`},{do:{friendship:`clara`,points:50}}]}]}]},{title:`A Heron in Oak`,summary:`Clara carved you a little oak heron, measured three times and sanded for weeks.`,lead:`You're courting Clara. Keep seeing her (10 hearts).`,cutscene:[{say:`clara`,text:`[shy] I made you something. It isn't finished. It's never finished. I had to stop sanding it eventually.`},{say:`narrator`,text:`A heron carved from oak, no taller than your hand, its wings half open, so smooth it felt like silk.`},{say:`clara`,text:`I measured every feather. Three times. I wanted it to be right, because it's for you.`},{say:`clara`,text:`[sad] My father used to say you make the things you love properly, or not at all.`},{choice:[{label:`It's perfect.`},{label:`Kiss her.`,then:[{say:`clara`,text:`[shy] ...Well. That wasn't measured at all. Do it again.`}]}]},{do:{flag:`key:heron-clara`}}]}],bouquet:[{say:`clara`,text:`[surprised] For me? These are... thank you. Nobody has given me flowers since I came back.`},{say:`clara`,text:`[shy] I've been wondering whether you'd ask. I measured it from every side and I still couldn't tell.`},{say:`clara`,text:`[happy] Yes. I would like that very much.`}],proposal:[{say:`clara`,text:`[surprised] A heron pendant. You... are you sure? Truly sure?`},{say:`player`,text:`I measured twice. Three times. I'm sure.`},{say:`clara`,text:`[laugh] ...Oh, you are impossible. Yes. Yes, of course yes.`}],vows:[{say:`clara`,text:`[shy] I came back to this island to set one thing right. I never thought I'd find something to build instead.`},{say:`clara`,text:`I'll build it slowly, and properly, for the rest of my life. I will.`}],afterVows:[{say:`clara`,text:`[happy] My father would have liked you. He would have made you hold the tape.`},{say:`clara`,text:`[laugh] Mrs... no. Still Clara. Just Clara, at Thistledown.`}],family:[`nell`],baby:[[{say:`clara`,text:`[surprised] A baby. I... yes. I've thought about it. I made a list. And then I built a cradle, just in case.`},{say:`clara`,text:`[shy] It's in the workshop, under a sheet. I've sanded it four times. Let's.`}],[{say:`clara`,text:`[happy] I was going to ask you. I've already started the second cradle.`},{say:`clara`,text:`[laugh] I measured {child} for it last night. Don't tell anyone I did that.`}]],newborn:[{say:`clara`,text:`[shy] Ten fingers. Ten toes. 